From: Velle Sinclair <brncomputerhelp@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fragment highp when the driver has it, not when it says so

Every fragment shader here opens with

    #ifdef GL_FRAGMENT_PRECISION_HIGH
    precision highp float;
    #else
    precision mediump float;
    #endif

and on NVIDIA's GLES2 that macro is NOT defined, while
glGetShaderPrecisionFormat(GL_FRAGMENT_SHADER, GL_HIGH_FLOAT) answers with a
full 23-bit mantissa. The macro means "advertised", not "supported", so the
whole renderer silently compiled at mediump — fp16, which is exact only to
2048 and then steps in 2s.

That is a rendering bug for any output wider than 2048. corner_alpha() compares
absolute fragment coordinates against the rect and its clipped region:

    vec2 relative_pos = (gl_FragCoord.xy - position);
    if (relative_pos.x > size.x) { ... }

At x = 2558 on a 2560-wide output the two sides of that comparison round to the
same fp16 value, the pixel is judged to be inside the clipped-out area, and the
fragment is discarded. A window's 2 px border ring is exactly that wide, so a
maximized window on a 2560x1440 output loses its entire right border while the
left, top and bottom (all below 2048) are perfect. The rounded corners survive,
because near a corner corner_alpha() takes the SDF branch instead of the plain
inside/outside one.

Measured on an RTX 3060, GLES2 610.57.04, rendering this exact shader with
synui's uniforms (2560x1440 rect, 2 px ring, outer radius 8) and reading the
mid-height row back:

    width   right-hand columns lit
     1024   yes        2044  yes        2048  yes        2050  yes
     2056   NO         2560  NO         3840  NO         7680  NO

llvmpipe renders the same call correctly, which is what makes this look like a
compositor bug rather than a shader one.

Fixed by asking the driver rather than the preprocessor: link_program() probes
glGetShaderPrecisionFormat once and prepends `#define FX_HAS_HIGHP 1` to every
fragment source when fragment highp is genuinely available, and the guards
honour it alongside the standard macro. Hardware with no fragment highp still
gets mediump, exactly as before. With the fix the same sweep is "yes" at every
width through 7680.

This is not only the border: every rounded corner, shadow, blur clip region and
texture sample past x=2048 was being placed on a 2 px grid.

diff -ruN a/render/fx_renderer/shaders/blur_effects.frag b/render/fx_renderer/shaders/blur_effects.frag
--- a/render/fx_renderer/shaders/blur_effects.frag	2026-06-28 00:36:32.000000000 -0500
+++ b/render/fx_renderer/shaders/blur_effects.frag	2026-08-18 22:46:04.957903859 -0500
@@ -1,4 +1,9 @@
-#ifdef GL_FRAGMENT_PRECISION_HIGH
+/* SynapseOS: NVIDIA's GLES2 supports fragment highp (glGetShaderPrecisionFormat
+ * reports 23 mantissa bits) but does NOT define GL_FRAGMENT_PRECISION_HIGH, so
+ * this guard silently fell through to mediump — fp16, exact only to 2048 — and
+ * every coordinate past that quantised to a 2 px grid. FX_HAS_HIGHP is defined
+ * by link_program() after asking the driver. See the 0002 patch header. */
+#if defined(GL_FRAGMENT_PRECISION_HIGH) || defined(FX_HAS_HIGHP)
 precision highp float;
 #else
 precision mediump float;
